1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
This is Doing and Feeling.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:04,890
Part two: feeling. Episode seven:

3
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:11,030
The Hard Problem. In 1714, Gottfried Leibniz
proposed a thought experiment that sits

4
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:13,600
underneath every argument
in this part of the series.

5
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:19,070
Suppose, he said, there were a machine
whose structure made it think, feel, and

6
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:24,570
perceive. Now imagine it enlarged, keeping
all its proportions, until you could walk

7
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:26,620
inside it the way you walk into a mill.

8
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:30,700
Inside, you would find only parts
pushing against other parts.

9
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:34,000
Nowhere would you find anything
that explains a perception.

10
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:39,680
Leibniz concluded that perception must
belong to something simple, not to mechanism.

11
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:44,260
Swap the mill for a modern neural network
and you have the contemporary version.

12
00:00:44,740 --> 00:00:49,270
Open up a large language model and
you find matrix multiplications and

13
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,080
nonlinearities all the way down.

14
00:00:51,590 --> 00:00:55,690
You can trace every number it computes,
and nowhere in the trace is there a place

15
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:57,400
where things start to feel like something.

16
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:03,210
Open up a brain and the story is the same,
with ion channels and neurotransmitters

17
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:08,030
instead of matrices. Notice the shape
of the argument, because it's this whole

18
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:12,870
episode in miniature. It moves from "I
cannot see experience in the mechanism" to

19
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:15,240
"experience is not in the mechanism."

20
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:19,780
The question for this episode is whether
that move is valid, and what you're

21
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:22,120
committed to if you say yes, or no.

22
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:27,160
Here's the route. A short history of
how this became a respectable problem.

23
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:30,640
Then the three arguments that
define the modern debate:

24
00:01:31,020 --> 00:01:36,430
Thomas Nagel's bat, Joseph Levine's
explanatory gap, and David Chalmers's hard

25
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:41,290
problem, including the formal conceivability
argument and the full map of responses

26
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:45,660
to it. Then Frank Jackson's Mary
and the four serious replies to her.

27
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:50,550
Then the distinction between access and
phenomenal consciousness, and an experiment

28
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:53,320
from 1960 that still divides the field.

29
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:58,930
Then Wittgenstein. And finally a first
look at the meta-problem, which the next

30
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:03,700
episode puts to work. The problem
has been noticed many times.

31
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:08,610
In the eighteen sixties, Thomas Huxley
wrote that the arrival of a state of

32
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:13,390
consciousness from irritated nerve
tissue was as unaccountable as the genie

33
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:15,450
appearing when Aladdin rubbed his lamp.

34
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:22,150
In 1872, the physiologist Emil du Bois-Reymond
gave a famous lecture on the limits

35
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:25,460
of natural science, and
consciousness was on his list.

36
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:31,300
His verdict on how matter produces sensation
was a single Latin word, ignorabimus:

37
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:36,250
we shall never know. Then psychology spent
much of the twentieth century avoiding

38
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:41,710
the subject. Behaviorism said that science
should study behavior, not inner states.

39
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:47,070
In 1949, the philosopher Gilbert Ryle
mocked the picture of a ghost in the machine

40
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:52,190
as a category mistake, like a visitor who
is shown the colleges, the libraries, and

41
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:55,480
the laboratories, and then
asks where the university is.

42
00:02:55,640 --> 00:03:00,390
The cognitive revolution brought back
internal processing, but as information

43
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:03,940
processing. Experience
itself stayed off the agenda.

44
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:07,050
The modern revival came
in the nineteen nineties.

45
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:13,230
In 1994, Francis Crick, co-discoverer
of the structure of DNA, published The

46
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:18,730
Astonishing Hypothesis, arguing that your
joys and sorrows, your memories, your

47
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:23,210
sense of self and free will, come down
to the activity of enormous numbers of

48
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:28,750
neurons. That same year, at a conference
in Tucson, a young Australian philosopher

49
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:33,020
named David Chalmers gave a talk that
named the problem in a way that stuck.

50
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:38,090
Within a few years there were dedicated
journals, conferences, and a research

51
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:41,940
program. But the argument everyone
reaches for first is older.

52
00:03:43,080 --> 00:03:49,470
Thomas Nagel's 1974 essay, What Is It Like
to Be a Bat, is sixteen pages long and

53
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:52,840
makes four moves. The fourth
is the one people forget.

54
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:58,810
First, a definition. A creature is conscious,
Nagel says, when there is something it

55
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:01,520
is like to be that creature,
for the creature itself.

56
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:06,290
That phrase, something it is like, became
the standard way of picking out what

57
00:04:06,340 --> 00:04:08,600
philosophers call
phenomenal consciousness.

58
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:12,600
Second, that subjective character
is tied to a point of view.

59
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,300
Bats perceive the world by echolocation:

60
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:19,060
they emit high-pitched calls and
build a picture from the echoes.

61
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:21,690
We can study sonar exhaustively.

62
00:04:21,740 --> 00:04:26,310
But what it is like to perceive the world
that way is fully accessible only from a

63
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:27,560
bat's kind of point of view.

64
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:32,280
Imagining yourself hanging upside
down with webbed arms doesn't help.

65
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:36,610
That tells you what it would be like for
you to behave like a bat, not what it is

66
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:38,000
like for a bat to be a bat.

67
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:43,750
Third, and this is the key step, scientific
reduction normally works by moving away

68
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:47,300
from particular points of view
toward more objective description.

69
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:52,670
We understood lightning by setting aside
how it looks to human eyes and describing

70
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:57,190
an electrical discharge. But if the thing
you're trying to explain is the point of

71
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:02,030
view itself, then moving away from points
of view moves you away from the very thing

72
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:04,960
you wanted to explain.
Fourth, the conclusion.

73
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:07,900
Nagel did not say physicalism is false.

74
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:11,320
He said that we currently have
no idea how it could be true.

75
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:16,730
His analogy was the claim that matter is
energy, put forward by a philosopher in

76
00:05:16,780 --> 00:05:21,210
ancient Greece. It might have been true,
but no one then had the concepts to see

77
00:05:21,260 --> 00:05:26,520
how. Nagel even proposed a research
program he called objective phenomenology:

78
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:31,800
a way of describing the structure of
experiences that doesn't require having them.

79
00:05:32,040 --> 00:05:37,130
That program is still mostly a promissory
note, but it's worth remembering that the

80
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:42,210
most famous anti-reductionist essay of
the twentieth century ends by asking for

81
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:45,160
better science, not by
declaring it impossible.

82
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:50,610
Joseph Levine sharpened the point
in 1983 with a phrase that stuck:

83
00:05:50,660 --> 00:05:54,540
the explanatory gap.
Compare two identity claims.

84
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:56,840
Heat is the motion of molecules.

85
00:05:57,300 --> 00:06:02,610
And pain is the firing of C fibers,
which, by the way, is a philosophers' toy

86
00:06:02,660 --> 00:06:04,920
example rather than real neuroscience.

87
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:09,480
In the heat case, once you understand
molecular motion, nothing is left over.

88
00:06:09,940 --> 00:06:12,200
Molecular motion does
everything heat does:

89
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:15,460
it raises thermometers,
melts ice, burns your hand.

90
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:17,220
There's no residual question.

91
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:21,400
In the pain case, even if the identity
were true, you could still ask:

92
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:25,350
why does that firing feel like this,
rather than like something else, or like

93
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:28,980
nothing at all? The identity might
hold, but it doesn't explain.

94
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:32,620
Levine's point was
epistemic, not metaphysical.

95
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:35,790
He was not claiming that
pain is non-physical.

96
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:40,470
He was claiming that our explanations leave
a gap that our explanations of heat do

97
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:45,290
not. Whether that gap reflects something
about the world, or only about the limits

98
00:06:45,340 --> 00:06:48,430
of our concepts, is what the
rest of the debate is about.

99
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:52,700
Heat had a gap too, until
statistical mechanics closed it.

100
00:06:52,980 --> 00:06:55,100
Why think this one is different in kind?

101
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:59,440
That's the hope of a position we'll
meet shortly, type-C physicalism.

102
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:01,920
The worry is about how
the heat gap closed.

103
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:06,240
Statistical mechanics showed that
molecular motion does everything heat does.

104
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:10,600
It explained heat's functions, and for
heat there was nothing more to explain.

105
00:07:10,940 --> 00:07:15,730
If experience is something over and above
its functions, a story about functions

106
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:17,820
can't close the gap the same way.

107
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:21,900
Whether it is something more is exactly
what Chalmers's argument is about.

108
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:24,680
Which brings us to Chalmers.

109
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:31,720
In his 1995 paper, Facing Up to the Problem
of Consciousness, and his 1996 book,

110
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:36,410
The Conscious Mind, he separated the easy
problems of consciousness from the hard

111
00:07:36,460 --> 00:07:39,840
problem. The easy problems are
about explaining functions:

112
00:07:40,180 --> 00:07:45,730
how we discriminate stimuli, integrate
information, report our mental states, focus

113
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:50,000
attention, deliberately control behavior,
and how waking differs from sleep.

114
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:52,540
They're easy only in a special sense.

115
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:56,670
They may take centuries to solve, but
we know what kind of thing an answer is:

116
00:07:56,720 --> 00:07:59,040
a mechanism that performs the function.

117
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:03,490
The hard problem is why the performance
of those functions is accompanied by

118
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:06,560
experience. Chalmers's
claim is structural.

119
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,750
Once you've explained every function, it
still seems a further question why any of

120
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:15,130
it is felt. Function doesn't
appear to entail feeling.

121
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:17,632
That sounds like an
argument from incredulity.

122
00:08:17,682 --> 00:08:22,995
I can't see how the code produces the
behavior, so the behavior must come from

123
00:08:23,045 --> 00:08:28,715
somewhere else. If a junior engineer said
that about a bug, I'd send them back to

124
00:08:28,765 --> 00:08:32,680
the debugger. And if that were the
whole argument, you'd be right to.

125
00:08:32,940 --> 00:08:36,660
So Chalmers made it explicit, and
it's worth going through slowly.

126
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,260
This is the conceivability argument.

127
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:42,440
Premise one. A zombie
world is conceivable.

128
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:47,450
Picture a world physically identical to
ours, down to the last particle, in which

129
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:49,080
nobody experiences anything.

130
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:54,000
Your zombie twin talks about the redness
of sunsets and the pain of stubbed toes,

131
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:56,040
but there's nothing it's like to be it.

132
00:08:56,260 --> 00:09:01,710
Not conceivable in a lazy sense, but
ideally conceivable, surviving any amount of

133
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:04,190
careful reflection. Premise two.

134
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:08,300
If something is ideally conceivable,
then it's metaphysically possible.

135
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:13,570
Premise three. If a zombie world is
possible, then the physical facts don't

136
00:09:13,620 --> 00:09:17,360
necessitate the facts about
experience, and physicalism is false.

137
00:09:17,940 --> 00:09:20,020
Conclusion: physicalism is false.

138
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:25,470
The argument is valid, so every response
has to reject a premise, and Chalmers

139
00:09:25,520 --> 00:09:31,220
himself drew the map of responses in a
2003 paper that sorts positions by letter.

140
00:09:31,540 --> 00:09:34,950
It's the most useful map in
the field, so here it is.

141
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,250
Type-A physicalists deny premise one.

142
00:09:38,300 --> 00:09:40,460
Daniel Dennett and the
illusionists live here.

143
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:45,210
Zombies only seem conceivable, they
say, because we haven't really imagined

144
00:09:45,260 --> 00:09:46,660
everything we're stipulating.

145
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:49,300
Dennett's comparison is vitalism.

146
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:54,690
Picture someone who, after every function
of life has been explained, still asks why

147
00:09:54,740 --> 00:09:56,880
all that is accompanied by life itself.

148
00:09:57,120 --> 00:10:01,520
The question sounds deep until you realize
that being alive just is metabolism,

149
00:10:02,040 --> 00:10:04,200
growth, reproduction, and the rest.

150
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:07,430
Once those are explained,
nothing is left over.

151
00:10:07,480 --> 00:10:10,240
On this view, experience is the same.

152
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:15,090
Explain every function and there's no
further ingredient, and zombies seem

153
00:10:15,140 --> 00:10:19,110
conceivable only because we never really
imagine all the functions at once.

154
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:23,870
Type-B physicalists accept
premise one but deny premise two.

155
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:27,440
Brian Loar, David Papineau,
and Ned Block are among them.

156
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:32,990
Their model is water. Before modern chemistry,
it seemed perfectly conceivable that

157
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:35,410
water was something other than H two O.

158
00:10:35,460 --> 00:10:41,510
But Saul Kripke showed that identity
statements like water is H two O, if true, are

159
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:44,888
necessarily true, even though
we discover them empirically.

160
00:10:44,938 --> 00:10:46,577
So conceivability can mislead.

161
00:10:46,627 --> 00:10:52,066
Maybe pain is identical to some brain
process necessarily, even though we can

162
00:10:52,116 --> 00:10:55,760
conceive otherwise. That
sounds like the obvious answer.

163
00:10:56,500 --> 00:11:00,200
Conceivability is a fact about
our concepts, not about the world.

164
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:03,680
So what stops type-B
physicalism from simply winning?

165
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:07,150
Partly Kripke himself,
which is a lovely twist.

166
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:11,400
In Naming and Necessity, he argued
that pain is not like water.

167
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:15,220
With water, we can explain away
the feeling of contingency.

168
00:11:15,420 --> 00:11:20,410
When we seem to imagine water that isn't
H two O, what we're really imagining is

169
00:11:20,460 --> 00:11:24,980
some watery-looking stuff that isn't
H two O, fool's water, so to speak.

170
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:26,800
With pain, the trick fails.

171
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:29,820
Anything that feels like
pain simply is pain.

172
00:11:30,120 --> 00:11:34,830
There's no gap between how pain appears and
what it is, so you cannot say you merely

173
00:11:34,880 --> 00:11:37,100
imagined something that seemed like pain.

174
00:11:37,640 --> 00:11:42,070
Kripke used this against identity
theories, and type-B physicalists have been

175
00:11:42,120 --> 00:11:43,440
working around it ever since.

176
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:48,990
Chalmers added a formal tool, two-dimensional
semantics, to say exactly when

177
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:51,240
conceivability does entail possibility.

178
00:11:51,700 --> 00:11:56,610
Very roughly, it separates what a word picks
out in the actual world from the way it

179
00:11:56,660 --> 00:12:01,850
picks things out. Type-B physicalists reject
the tool, and the dispute continues in

180
00:12:01,900 --> 00:12:07,530
technical journals. Type-C physicalism says
the gap is real now but will close once

181
00:12:07,580 --> 00:12:09,680
we have concepts we don't yet possess.

182
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:14,300
Chalmers argues that under pressure it
collapses into one of the other letters,

183
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:18,490
physicalist or not. Then come
the non-physicalist letters.

184
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:21,340
Type-D is interactionist dualism:

185
00:12:21,620 --> 00:12:24,750
experience is non-physical
and pushes matter around.

186
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:27,280
Type-E is epiphenomenalism:

187
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:30,800
experience is non-physical
and does nothing at all.

188
00:12:31,020 --> 00:12:36,480
And type-F is Russellian monism, the family
that includes many forms of panpsychism,

189
00:12:36,680 --> 00:12:41,550
on which experience is the hidden intrinsic
nature of what physics describes only

190
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:44,770
from the outside. Keep
these six letters in mind.

191
00:12:44,820 --> 00:12:50,050
Type-A gets the whole of the next episode,
and the rest are the skeleton of episode

192
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:56,250
nine. Frank Jackson's knowledge argument,
from 1982, approaches the same territory

193
00:12:56,300 --> 00:13:01,130
from another direction. Mary is a brilliant
scientist who knows every physical fact

194
00:13:01,180 --> 00:13:05,440
about color vision: the wavelengths,
the retinal cones, every neural pathway.

195
00:13:05,620 --> 00:13:08,520
But she has lived her whole
life in a black and white room.

196
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:11,260
One day she walks out
and sees a ripe tomato.

197
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:12,900
Does she learn something new?

198
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:17,080
If she does, then knowing all the physical
facts was not knowing all the facts.

199
00:13:17,400 --> 00:13:20,510
There are four main replies, and
they're worth having straight.

200
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:24,730
The ability hypothesis, from
David Lewis and Laurence Nemirow.

201
00:13:24,780 --> 00:13:27,660
Mary gains abilities rather than facts:

202
00:13:27,920 --> 00:13:31,490
the ability to imagine red, to
recognize it, to remember it.

203
00:13:31,540 --> 00:13:36,210
Know-how, not know-that. The objection
is that it really does seem she learns

204
00:13:36,260 --> 00:13:39,100
something statable: that
seeing red is like this.

205
00:13:39,260 --> 00:13:41,190
The acquaintance hypothesis.

206
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:45,900
Mary becomes directly acquainted with
a property she already knew all about.

207
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:48,100
A new relation to an old property.

208
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:52,340
Then the most influential reply,
the phenomenal concept strategy.

209
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:57,410
Mary acquires a new concept, a recognitional
concept that points at the experience

210
00:13:57,460 --> 00:14:00,260
from the inside, something
like "this kind of state."

211
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:04,520
That new concept picks out a physical
property she already knew under a different,

212
00:14:04,820 --> 00:14:09,390
scientific concept, the way the morning
star and the evening star turned out to be

213
00:14:09,440 --> 00:14:13,140
the same planet. Same fact,
new way of thinking about it.

214
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:15,460
The strategy has a second attraction.

215
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:20,510
If phenomenal concepts and physical
concepts are wired up independently in our

216
00:14:20,560 --> 00:14:25,610
heads, you'd expect to be able to pull them
apart in imagination, even if the things

217
00:14:25,660 --> 00:14:27,360
they refer to cannot come apart.

218
00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:32,770
So the strategy explains why zombies seem
conceivable without adding anything to the

219
00:14:32,820 --> 00:14:36,360
world. It explains the intuition
rather than honoring it.

220
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:39,900
That's the move that feels
right to me as an engineer.

221
00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:44,200
When a model is confidently
wrong, you explain the confidence.

222
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:46,350
You don't treat it as evidence.

223
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:49,220
It's attractive for exactly that reason.

224
00:14:49,880 --> 00:14:55,150
Chalmers replied in 2007 with what's
sometimes called his master argument, which is

225
00:14:55,200 --> 00:15:00,420
a dilemma. Ask whether your zombie twin
has the same phenomenal concepts you have.

226
00:15:00,580 --> 00:15:05,640
If it does, then those concepts cannot
explain your special epistemic situation,

227
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:08,600
since the zombie has them
without any experience.

228
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:13,830
If it does not, then phenomenal concepts
cannot be explained in physical terms after

229
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:17,340
all. Either way, he
argues, the strategy fails.

230
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:22,820
Its defenders have replies, and this is
one of the liveliest disputes in the field.

231
00:15:23,140 --> 00:15:24,940
The fourth reply is Dennett's.

232
00:15:25,180 --> 00:15:29,230
He argued that a Mary who truly knew
everything physical would not be surprised at

233
00:15:29,280 --> 00:15:33,180
all. Show her a blue banana as
a trick, and she'd say at once:

234
00:15:33,380 --> 00:15:35,300
that is not what yellow looks like.

235
00:15:35,480 --> 00:15:40,370
And one more twist: Jackson himself
later abandoned the argument and became a

236
00:15:40,420 --> 00:15:44,420
physicalist, explaining experience
in terms of mental representation.

237
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:47,640
The argument outlived its
author's belief in it.

238
00:15:48,680 --> 00:15:52,900
Next, a distinction that quietly
causes half the confusion in this area.

239
00:15:53,260 --> 00:15:59,190
In 1995, Ned Block distinguished phenomenal
consciousness, the what-it's-like, from

240
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:04,670
access consciousness: information being
available for reasoning, for report, and for

241
00:16:04,720 --> 00:16:09,575
controlling behavior. Block's point was
that the two are conceptually distinct, and

242
00:16:09,625 --> 00:16:12,480
that scientists constantly
slide between them.

243
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:17,330
His example was blindsight, which we
will meet properly in episode ten:

244
00:16:17,380 --> 00:16:22,130
people with damage to visual cortex who
deny seeing anything in part of their visual

245
00:16:22,180 --> 00:16:25,820
field, yet can guess about objects
there far better than chance.

246
00:16:25,980 --> 00:16:31,010
It's tempting to reason that because these
patients lack visual experience and also

247
00:16:31,060 --> 00:16:35,380
lack certain abilities, experience
must be what supplies those abilities.

248
00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:37,480
Block called this a fallacy.

249
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:39,830
The patients also lack access.

250
00:16:39,880 --> 00:16:43,900
You cannot tell which missing ingredient
explains the missing abilities.

251
00:16:44,560 --> 00:16:49,000
Block then went further and argued that
phenomenal consciousness overflows access.

252
00:16:49,360 --> 00:16:53,780
His evidence starts with an experiment
by George Sperling from 1960.

253
00:16:54,400 --> 00:16:59,360
Flash a grid of twelve letters, three
rows of four, for a fraction of a second.

254
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:03,020
Ask people to report everything,
and they manage about four.

255
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:08,250
But if a tone just after the flash tells
them which row to report, they can get most

256
00:17:08,300 --> 00:17:10,520
of that row, whichever row it is.

257
00:17:11,080 --> 00:17:15,550
So for a moment, most of the grid was
available, even though only a fraction can

258
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:20,320
ever be reported. Block's interpretation
is that you consciously see the whole grid,

259
00:17:20,600 --> 00:17:23,820
specific letters and all, and
that what's limited is access.

260
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:26,460
Experience is richer than report.

261
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:32,790
He made the case in 2007 in a target article
in Behavioral and Brain Sciences, the

262
00:17:32,840 --> 00:17:37,530
format we met in episode five, and its
commentaries are a fine place to watch a

263
00:17:37,580 --> 00:17:42,490
field argue with itself. On the other side,
Michael Cohen and Daniel Dennett argued

264
00:17:42,540 --> 00:17:47,260
in 2011 that experiences inaccessible
in principle could never be tested.

265
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:52,590
Others, such as the philosopher Ian Phillips,
suggested that people may experience

266
00:17:52,640 --> 00:17:56,940
something more generic: letter-like
shapes rather than specific letters.

267
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:01,460
Underneath this lies what's often
called the refrigerator light problem.

268
00:18:01,720 --> 00:18:05,230
Whenever you check whether you're
conscious of something, you access it.

269
00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:10,650
So you can never catch experience in the
act of existing without access, just as you

270
00:18:10,700 --> 00:18:13,400
can never open the fridge
door and see the light off.

271
00:18:13,700 --> 00:18:17,380
So this is a measurement problem
wearing a metaphysics costume.

272
00:18:17,680 --> 00:18:20,640
Every probe you can
build is an access probe.

273
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:26,990
Largely, yes. And it becomes a major
empirical fight in episode eleven, because two

274
00:18:27,040 --> 00:18:30,500
leading scientific theories
line up on opposite sides of it.

275
00:18:30,700 --> 00:18:33,890
A few more distinctions will keep
the rest of the series clear.

276
00:18:33,940 --> 00:18:38,850
Philosophers separate creature consciousness,
being awake rather than in a coma,

277
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:43,530
from state consciousness, a particular
mental state being conscious rather than

278
00:18:43,580 --> 00:18:49,030
unconscious. Scientists separate the level
of consciousness, its contents, and the

279
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:54,030
conscious self. And some researchers,
including Tim Bayne, argue that even talking

280
00:18:54,080 --> 00:18:55,480
about levels is misleading.

281
00:18:56,040 --> 00:19:01,630
Dreaming, psychedelic states, sedation, and
minimal consciousness after brain injury

282
00:19:01,680 --> 00:19:06,230
differ along several dimensions at once,
rather than sitting at different points on

283
00:19:06,280 --> 00:19:10,980
one dial. Ludwig Wittgenstein
enters the story from an angle.

284
00:19:11,260 --> 00:19:16,170
In the Philosophical Investigations he
imagines that everyone has a box containing

285
00:19:16,220 --> 00:19:17,440
something they call a beetle.

286
00:19:17,960 --> 00:19:22,550
No one can look into anyone else's box,
and everyone knows what a beetle is only

287
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:27,470
from their own case. His point is that in
such a language game, the thing in the box

288
00:19:27,520 --> 00:19:29,180
drops out of the picture entirely.

289
00:19:29,700 --> 00:19:35,230
The word beetle gets its meaning from its
public use, whatever is or is not inside.

290
00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:38,540
That image belongs to his larger
private language argument:

291
00:19:38,840 --> 00:19:43,410
the claim that words for sensations cannot
get their meaning from private inner

292
00:19:43,460 --> 00:19:46,260
objects that only the speaker can inspect.

293
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:51,190
And in a passage that every student of
this problem should read, he describes the

294
00:19:51,240 --> 00:19:55,280
feeling of an unbridgeable gulf between
consciousness and brain processes.

295
00:19:55,580 --> 00:19:58,420
It doesn't arise in
ordinary life, he says.

296
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:03,070
It arises when you turn your attention on
your own consciousness, in a philosophical

297
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:07,730
mood, and ask in astonishment how this
could possibly come from a process in the

298
00:20:07,780 --> 00:20:12,060
brain. Notice the word this, and the
gesture inward that goes with it.

299
00:20:12,780 --> 00:20:17,590
Wittgenstein treated that astonishment as
a symptom to be examined, not a discovery

300
00:20:17,640 --> 00:20:23,120
to be honored. Wittgenstein's influence
runs through behaviorism, through Dennett,

301
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:26,360
and into the contemporary
view called illusionism.

302
00:20:26,520 --> 00:20:28,980
He didn't try to solve the hard problem.

303
00:20:29,140 --> 00:20:33,030
He tried to show that it might
be a knot in our language.

304
00:20:33,080 --> 00:20:37,710
That astonishment, the inward gesture and
the sense of a gulf, is itself something

305
00:20:37,760 --> 00:20:42,120
people do. They say it, write it
down, and argue about it on podcasts.

306
00:20:42,600 --> 00:20:46,900
In 2018, Chalmers turned that
observation into a research program.

307
00:20:47,540 --> 00:20:50,950
Alongside the hard problem,
he proposed the meta-problem:

308
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,500
explain why we think
there is a hard problem.

309
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:57,900
Why do brains produce reports like
"I can't explain my own experience"?

310
00:20:58,440 --> 00:21:03,430
Because those reports are behavior,
explaining them is, in principle, an easy

311
00:21:03,480 --> 00:21:08,110
problem, one that realists and their
opponents can work on together while expecting

312
00:21:08,160 --> 00:21:09,360
different kinds of answer.

313
00:21:09,700 --> 00:21:14,250
I think it's the most productive development
in this debate in decades, and the next

314
00:21:14,300 --> 00:21:19,680
episode builds on it. Let me step back
and name the strategies on the table.

315
00:21:19,940 --> 00:21:22,060
The first is to dissolve the problem:

316
00:21:22,340 --> 00:21:25,220
show that it rests on a
confusion or an illusion.

317
00:21:25,900 --> 00:21:30,090
That's Wittgenstein's spirit, and the
illusionist program of Dennett and Keith

318
00:21:30,140 --> 00:21:33,060
Frankish. The second is
to reduce the problem:

319
00:21:33,440 --> 00:21:38,480
accept that experience is real and physical,
and explain why it seems otherwise.

320
00:21:38,980 --> 00:21:43,490
That's type-B physicalism, the
phenomenal concept strategy, and the

321
00:21:43,540 --> 00:21:48,290
representationalist theories that identify
experience with a certain kind of mental

322
00:21:48,340 --> 00:21:52,220
representation. The third is to
expand our picture of reality:

323
00:21:52,760 --> 00:21:56,440
accept that physics as we know it
leaves something out, and add to it.

324
00:21:56,940 --> 00:21:58,860
Dualism adds a new kind of property.

325
00:21:59,540 --> 00:22:04,900
Panpsychism and Russellian monism say
experience is the intrinsic nature of matter.

326
00:22:05,520 --> 00:22:08,180
Idealism says experience is all there is.

327
00:22:08,520 --> 00:22:11,680
And where do professional
philosophers actually land?

328
00:22:11,820 --> 00:22:15,240
Is there a majority view?
There's survey data.

329
00:22:15,480 --> 00:22:21,190
In the 2020 PhilPapers survey of professional
philosophers, about sixty-two percent

330
00:22:21,240 --> 00:22:25,550
said there is a hard problem of consciousness,
and about thirty percent said there

331
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:30,910
is not. At the same time, about fifty-two
percent accepted or leaned toward

332
00:22:30,960 --> 00:22:35,590
physicalism about the mind, against
thirty-two percent for non-physicalism.

333
00:22:35,640 --> 00:22:40,050
On consciousness specifically, functionalism
was the most popular single view at

334
00:22:40,100 --> 00:22:45,890
about a third, then dualism at about a
fifth, with identity theory, panpsychism, and

335
00:22:45,940 --> 00:22:50,540
eliminativism trailing. Put that together
and you get the field's real position:

336
00:22:50,840 --> 00:22:56,190
most philosophers think the problem is
genuine, and a narrow majority still expect a

337
00:22:56,240 --> 00:22:59,440
physical answer. Few think
it has been delivered yet.

338
00:23:00,380 --> 00:23:03,860
Eliminativism is the one that
says consciousness doesn't exist?

339
00:23:04,140 --> 00:23:07,980
With under five percent, why give
it more airtime than the others?

340
00:23:08,340 --> 00:23:10,680
Because the small number hides two things.

341
00:23:11,240 --> 00:23:16,230
First, the label eliminativism sounds
like denying that you feel pain, which no

342
00:23:16,280 --> 00:23:18,080
serious defender of the view does.

343
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:23,190
What they deny is that experience has
the special private, ineffable qualities

344
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:28,330
philosophers call qualia. Critics reply
that this is denying consciousness by

345
00:23:28,380 --> 00:23:32,060
another name. Most defenders
prefer the label illusionism.

346
00:23:32,320 --> 00:23:35,940
Second, it's the position that most
changes the question we care about.

347
00:23:36,240 --> 00:23:41,070
If illusionism is right, asking whether a
machine is conscious stops being a mystery

348
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:45,800
and becomes a question you could, in
principle, answer by looking inside it.

349
00:23:46,020 --> 00:23:51,370
So next episode, we give the most radical
answer the strongest hearing we can, and

350
00:23:51,420 --> 00:23:53,360
then put it through
its hardest objections.
